This project will introduce a new hybrid query language for arbitrary
"map-like" data structures: JSON primarily, but also the JSON-compatible
subset of YAML.

It will apply several standards:

- JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
- JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396)
- JSON Path (RFC 9535)

The mechanism for doing so is a s-expression based format that specifies
an operation over the document.


JSON PATCH

This is the most direct application of a standard:

(patch replace
    (path "/FirstName")
    (value "Jane"))

(patch remove
    (path "/Email"))

(patch add
    (path "/Address/ZipCode")
    (value "90210"))


JSON MERGE

Original document:

{
  "title": "Goodbye!",
  "author": {
    "givenName": "John",
    "familyName": "Doe"
  },
  "tags": [ "example", "sample" ],
  "content": "This will be unchanged"
}

Patch content:

{
  "title": "Hello!",
  "phoneNumber": "+01-123-456-7890",
  "author": {
    "familyName": null
  },
  "tags": [ "example" ]
}

Result:

{
  "title": "Hello!",
  "phoneNumber": "+01-123-456-7890",
  "author": {
    "givenName": "John"
  },
  "tags": [ "example" ],
  "content": "This will be unchanged"
}

In our language, this operation would be presented in s-expressions:

(merge (object
    (title . "Hello!")
    (phoneNumber . "+01-123-456-7890")
    (author . (object (familyName . NULL)))
    (tags . (array "example"))))

Or as a JSON "here document" that's parsed and semantically preserved
but not byte-identical:

(merge (JSON
    {
        "title": "Hello!",
        "phoneNumber": "+01-123-456-7890",
        "author": {
            "familyName": null
        },
        "tags": [ "example" ]
    }
END))

Note that the here document doesn't enable fence definition; it's always
JSON and END because it's definitionally unambiguous: everything between
them must be a single valid JSON value or will be rejected.

For similar reasons, indentation is not significant. JSON 42 END is a
perfectly valid "document."


JSON PATH

JSON Path is not a patch format, but instead a query language. As such,
it can be used in many cases where the JSON Patch and Merge Patch
formats leave something to be desired. It serves as a way to provide
multiple targets for a transformation:

{
    "a": {
        "sharedKey": true,
        "uniqueA": 1
    },
    "b": {
        "sharedKey": true,
        "uniqueB": 2
    },
    "c": {
        "sharedKey": false
    }
}

This query would identify and remove sharedKey values where they are
true:

(query "$..[?(@.sharedKey == true)]"
    (patch remove (path "/sharedKey")))

This results in:

{
    "a": {
        "uniqueA": 1
    },
    "b": {
        "uniqueB": 2
    },
    "c": {
        "sharedKey": false
    }
}

Similarly, it could be used with merge:

(query "$..[?(@.sharedKey == true)]"
    (merge (object (answer . 42))))

{
    "a": {
        "sharedKey": true,
        "uniqueA": 1,
        "answer": 42
    },
    "b": {
        "sharedKey": true,
        "uniqueB": 2,
        "answer": 42
    },
    "c": {
        "sharedKey": false
    }
}

Because JSONPath can produce duplicate nodes, it is normalized to paths
(e.g. /a and /b with this query) and de-duplicated before the
application of merges and patches.

Similarly, transformations are all-or-nothing: if nine succeed and one
fails, then the entire application fails. The intent of this format is
to be declarative and atomic.

Transforms are applied in the order that they occur and operate on the
document produced by the previous form. This can produce surprising
output in some cases.

To minimize surprises, nodes are totally ordered: at each step in a

1. Depth-first
2. Parent-before-children ordering
3. Keys ordered by their position within the input document
4. Later-added keys are ordered by their input order

fashion. JSONPath queries are ordered by this total ordering and are
fixed before mutation within one query form.

To avoid a common foot-gun, locations within one query form remain
fixed. For example, editing the array ["a", "b", "c", "d", "e"] to
remove indices 1 and 3 always removes the entries for "b" and "d".

Invalid targets are always errors, and an error message is presented
showing the transformation where the target was last valid for debugging
purposes.
